WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.612
ReListen: training-free evidence localization in
hour-long audio, with frozen audio language models.

00:00:09.012 --> 00:00:12.937
Here is an 84-minute recording,
and one question about it.

00:00:17.521 --> 00:00:20.486
The answer sits in about one minute of the audio.

00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:25.394
A transcript would make the hour fit,
but it throws away the voices and sounds.

00:00:25.694 --> 00:00:29.043
And most audio models cannot
hear a whole hour at once.

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:35.229
ReListen lets one frozen audio model
search the recording by itself.

00:00:35.529 --> 00:00:39.911
It cuts the audio into windows of
20 seconds, and asks of each one:

00:00:39.911 --> 00:00:43.443
does this excerpt help answer
the question? Yes or no.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:47.647
It reads the probability of yes,
without generating any text.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.931
The scores peak where the evidence is.

00:00:50.231 --> 00:00:55.281
The same model then listens again to its
three best windows, one minute in total,

00:00:55.281 --> 00:00:56.653
and answers correctly.

00:00:56.953 --> 00:00:58.254
Nothing is trained.

00:00:59.654 --> 00:01:06.865
On VoiceGiraffe, the selected minute overlaps
the annotated evidence for 88.5% of questions.

00:01:07.165 --> 00:01:10.663
Searching a transcript finds it for 58.8%.

00:01:10.963 --> 00:01:17.235
And answering from that one minute beats a
transcript of the whole recording by 9.2 points.

00:01:18.735 --> 00:01:21.445
Why listen, instead of
reading the selected minute?

00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:25.094
For questions about what is said,
reading works about as well.

00:01:25.394 --> 00:01:28.197
But for questions about sounds and voices,

00:01:28.197 --> 00:01:32.135
listening to the same excerpts
is 8.6 points more accurate.

00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:38.926
A frozen audio model can find its own
evidence. No transcript, and no training.
